# Ctrl-Z on a running *external* job (`cmd sleep 100`) must suspend it and return control
# to the nested crush session's own prompt, with the job showing up as Paused.
#
# `pty_send_control` (see pty_test_helpers.crush) sends the Ctrl-Z byte and, if it
# doesn't land -- the kernel's line discipline generates SIGTSTP for whichever process
# currently owns the terminal the instant it sees the byte, independent of whether
# `sleep`'s fork/exec/terminal-handoff has actually finished yet -- resends it rather
# than waiting out a fixed guess. Empirically (100+ runs, including under artificial
# CPU load during this investigation) a single send already lands reliably every time
# on this machine; the retry is a zero-fixed-cost safety net for the race described
# above, not a correction for something actually observed.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)

$t.write "cmd sleep 100\n"
$n = $(pty_send_control $t $n $pty.control.z)
pty_assert_job_status $t 0 "Paused"
$t.close
